[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Error Handling, Try-Catch Blocks, and Timeout Management in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Robust CI/CD pipelines require resilient error handling and precise timeout management to prevent stalled builds and resource leaks. In Jenkins Declarative and Scripted pipelines, implementing try-catch blocks and timeout mechanisms ensures your automation scripts fail gracefully and clean up resources effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Timeout Management"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Hanging builds can consume execution slots indefinitely. Jenkins provides a built-in timeout step to automatically abort a block of code if it exceeds a specified duration. You can configure timeouts using units like SECONDS, MINUTES, or HOURS."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                timeout(time: 10, unit: 'MINUTES') {
                    sh './deploy-script.sh'
                }
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Try-Catch Blocks in Scripted Pipelines"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Declarative pipelines use standard post-conditions, Scripted pipelines offer granular control over exceptions using traditional Groovy try-catch-finally blocks. This allows you to catch specific errors, log custom messages, and execute cleanup routines."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"node {
    stage('Build and Test') {
        try {
            echo 'Starting compilation...'
            sh 'mvn clean package'
        } catch (Exception e) {
            echo "Build failed with error: ${e.getMessage()}"
            currentBuild.result = 'FAILURE'
            throw e
        } finally {
            echo 'Cleaning up temporary files...'
            cleanWs()
        }
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling Best Practices"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always combine timeouts with critical steps like network requests, database migrations, or external deployments."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'finally' block to guarantee workspace cleanup, notification dispatches, or infrastructure teardowns regardless of build status."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Explicitly set 'currentBuild.result' when catching exceptions if you want the pipeline to record a failure state while continuing execution."}]
